    {
      "id": "PR_kwDOLyTYAM7m89XH",
      "title": "sdm: define intrinsic-warm exclusions for block-level warming rebates",
      "author": "nonsense",
      "number": 387,
      "body": "## Summary\n\nMakes the block-level warming policy precise about which accesses earn a rebate. Previously the doc said only *\"when a later tx accesses state already warmed earlier in the block, a rebate is accumulated\"* — which ambiguously includes a transaction's own intrinsically-warm accounts and led a producer implementation to over-refund (and drain the OP fee vaults).\n\nThis change:\n- States a rebate is owed **only when the tx actually pays the cold access cost** for state an earlier tx in the block warmed.\n- Adds **\"What is never rebated: intrinsically-warm and non-charged accesses\"** — the full EIP-2929/2930/3651/7702 intrinsic set (own `tx.sender`, own `tx.to` / created-contract address, precompiles, coinbase, access-list entries, 7702 authorities), the fee-vault settlement-write nuance (warm-for-later, never claim), and deposits.\n- Adds worked **examples**: same sender twice, two txs to one contract (no `to` rebate, but storage/third-account rebates survive), CREATE, and fee vaults.\n\nThe shared principle: *warming an account/slot for later transactions* and *claiming a rebate for it* are separate; a touch the EVM did not charge the cold price for claims nothing.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
      "repository": "ethereum-optimism/design-docs",
      "createdAt": "2026-06-16T10:13:53Z",
      "mergedAt": "2026-06-16T15:16:07Z",
      "additions": 26,
      "deletions": 7
    }
  ],
